@Tutorial(time: 25) {
    @Intro(title: "Calculate Yacht Scores") {
        This chapter focuses not on TabletopKit but on **pure Swift game rules**. You build a calculator that, given the current five dice results, works out how many points each Yacht category would score.
    }

    @Section(title: "Define the Score Categories") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Instead of using on-screen strings directly in conditionals, you represent categories with the `YachtCategory` enum. This is also where you distinguish the Numbers and Combinations sections.

            The scoring rules in this sample are as follows.

            | Category | How it's calculated |
            | --- | --- |
            | Ones...Sixes | The sum of the dice showing that number |
            | Choice | The sum of all five dice |
            | Four of a Kind | The sum of all dice if four or more show the same number |
            | Full House | The sum of all dice for a 2 + 3 combination |
            | Small Straight | 15 points if the condition is met |
            | Large Straight | 30 points if the condition is met |
            | Yacht | 50 points if all five dice show the same number |
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In Xcode, create a new Swift file named `YachtCategory.swift` and define the enum for the score categories and their display names. Define the 12 categories, their display names, and the target face for Numbers.
                @Code(name: "YachtCategory.swift", file: 07-01-YachtCategory.swift, reset: true)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Handle Every Category in a Single Calculator") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The score calculator doesn't read RealityKit or TabletopKit state directly. Because it takes only five `[Int]` values as input, it's easy to test and reuse.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In Xcode, create a new Swift file named `YachtScoreCalculator.swift` and define the type that handles the Numbers and Choice calculations. First, implement a minimal calculator that handles only Numbers and Choice.
                @Code(name: "YachtScoreCalculator.swift", file: 07-02-YachtScoreCalculator-Basic.swift, reset: true)
            }
            @Step {
                In the same `YachtScoreCalculator.swift`, **add the frequency calculation and the Four of a Kind / Full House branches** to the `score(dice:category:)` implementation. If you collect how many times each pip appeared in a `Dictionary`, both rules can reuse the **same frequency data**. Also notice how `reduce(into:)` builds this dictionary in a single pass over the array.
                @Code(name: "YachtScoreCalculator.swift", file: 07-03-YachtScoreCalculator-Groups.swift, previousFile: 07-02-YachtScoreCalculator-Basic.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                In Xcode, create a new Swift file named `YachtScoreCalculator+Patterns.swift` and write the helpers that recognize the Straight and Yacht pip patterns in an extension. Using `Set` and `isSubset(of:)`, you check for containment rather than order.
                @Code(name: "YachtScoreCalculator+Patterns.swift", file: 07-04-YachtScoreCalculator+Patterns.swift, reset: true)
            }
            @Step {
                Go back to `YachtScoreCalculator.swift`, **complete the remaining category branches of `score(dice:category:)` by calling the pattern helpers**, and add `scores(for:)`, which builds the expected score for every category.
                @Code(name: "YachtScoreCalculator.swift", file: 07-05-YachtScoreCalculator-All.swift, previousFile: 07-03-YachtScoreCalculator-Groups.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                Open `YachtGame.swift` and add a `YachtScoreCalculator` instance to the class's stored properties. The basic structure that creates the 3D scene stays as it is.
                @Code(name: "YachtGame.swift", file: 07-06-YachtGame-Scoring.swift, previousFile: 06-02-YachtGame-Hold.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                In Xcode, create a new Swift file named `YachtGame+Scoring.swift` and write, in an extension, the property that calculates the expected score for each category from the current dice results.
                @Code(name: "YachtGame+Scoring.swift", file: 07-07-YachtGame+Scoring-Preview.swift, reset: true)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Verify") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The calculator interprets the same dice results differently for each category, as shown below.

            | Dice | Result to check |
            | --- | --- |
            | `[6, 6, 6, 6, 2]` | Sixes 24, Four of a Kind 26 |
            | `[2, 2, 3, 3, 3]` | Full House 13 |
            | `[1, 2, 3, 4, 6]` | Small Straight 15 |

            `YachtScoreCalculator` doesn't depend on TabletopKit or RealityKit types; it takes only `[Int]`. Thanks to that, you can turn the results from the 3D interaction into pure game data and then calculate each category's expected score independently.

            The values calculated now are previews that change whenever the dice results change. In Yacht, the value must be locked in the moment you choose a category, so the next chapter adds a separate score sheet state.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Compare the three examples above with the input to `YachtScoreCalculator.score(dice:category:)` and check what value each rule returns. For example, passing `[2, 2, 3, 3, 3]` with `.fullHouse` should give 13, and passing `[1, 2, 3, 4, 6]` with `.smallStraight` should give 15. The image on the right shows the expected scores for `[6, 6, 6, 6, 2]` on the score sheet you build in the next chapter.
                @Image(
                    source: "7_Section 3_Step 1.png",
                    alt: "The score sheet showing the expected scores for the dice result [6, 6, 6, 6, 2]: Sixes 24, Choice 26, and Four of a Kind 26. The dice panel below shows four sixes and one two, with a total of 26."
                )
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            What's the biggest advantage of separating the score calculator from TabletopKit types?
            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Separating the scoring code automatically raises RealityKit's rendering frame rate.
                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Improving rendering performance isn't the direct goal of this separation.
                }
            }
            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                You can test and reuse the game rules independently using only the dice results in `[Int]` form.
                @Justification(reaction: "Correct.") {
                    You can verify the scoring rules without a 3D interaction or a TabletopKit session.
                }
            }
            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Swift can return a dictionary only if you make `YachtScoreCalculator` a separate type.
                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    It isn't a limitation of Swift's type system; it's a design that separates responsibilities.
                }
            }
        }
        @MultipleChoice {
            Why is a frequency map useful for judging a Full House?
            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Because you can sort each die's 3D position and treat nearby dice as the same number.
                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Scoring doesn't use spatial positions.
                }
            }
            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Because you can use the cube face's raw value directly as the score without applying a `FaceMap`.
                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    A frequency map counts how often each value appears in results that have already been converted to numbers.
                }
            }
            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Because you can count how many times each pip value appeared and directly compare occurrence patterns like `[2, 3]`.
                @Justification(reaction: "That's right.") {
                    It suits rules like Full House, where the number of identical values matters more than the values themselves.
                }
            }
        }
        @MultipleChoice {
            In the current implementation, what's the direct condition under which `yachtScorePreviews` returns an empty dictionary?
            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                When `hasRolled` is false, so valid results for all five dice aren't ready yet.
                @Justification(reaction: "Correct.") {
                    Preview scores are calculated only when there's a current roll result.
                }
            }
            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                When `rollCount` has reached the maximum of 3.
                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Even after the third roll, you can show the score preview as long as there's a current result.
                }
            }
            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                When all five dice are held.
                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Even if they're all held, you can still calculate the score preview as long as the dice results have already been read.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
